00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.020
In the 90s when people started doing animation, it's like

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.060
"Oh, I can just make a 3D model and I'll put bones underneath which'll morph and stretch and move the model."

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:42.940
Simple enough, right?

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.660
Well, once we tried starting to make things photorealistic, we suddenly realized that that didn't look real.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.040
Because that's not how our skin moves; our skin doesn't just slide around.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:54.560
We have muscles underneath our skin and the muscles, when they're tight, they're gonna bulge out.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.160
And when they're loose they're gonna sag, they're gonna cause wrinkles and our skin is just on top of this...

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:01.180
Web of muscles and tendons that are pulling things around.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:05.320
So for something like Two-Face, you need that complexity of the muscles underneath pulling.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.080
And you can actually see the tendons sliding against each other - and it's all hand animated by the way.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.220
It's just a testament to us starting to understand how to animate humans and human faces.

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.140
What did they have like any tracking markers? What do they do there?

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.600
[Niko] So Christopher Nolan likes to shoot his movies with a lot of darkness, right?

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.240
And you've probably seen facial tracking where they have little black dots in the face.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.549
You're not going to see the black dots on his face when he's in shadow.

00:02:24.549 --> 00:02:27.660
So they're using little white markers, but even then those are in shadow, right?

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.860
They fall into shadow and you lose them.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.240
So what they're actually doing to track his face - it's not the main camera that's tracking his face.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:35.160
They actually have other cameras on set.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.200
And they have little ring lights around them that are very very faint - and that's all retro reflective material.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.880
All those other cameras are seeing nice vibrant bright dots even when it's in shadow because of the retro reflective material.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.380
But the main camera isn't seeing that at all.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:53.420
It's a really ingenious way to have motion tracking dots that you can see with multiple cameras, but not with the main camera.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.360
Another cool thing is when you're using multiple cameras

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.600
You can triangulate the positions of those dots and get 3d motion tracking data out of it

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:02.400
So that's how they're tracking his face to put the 3d model on it.

